{
  "analysis": "All files have $SPILLOVER in their header! But wait! Let's carefully read the acquisition notes:\n\"Experiment template: created on 26-MAY-2024, when the panel was first set up on this instrument; its compensation values date from that set-up. The detector voltages of the panel were re-optimised after the laser service of 04-JAN-2025. The compensation setup of the acquisition software was not run for this session; the experiment was recorded with the template compensation and exported as FCS 3.1 (the export stores the detector signals as recorded and the template values in the file header).\"\n\"After PBMC_D829 the HLA-DR signal of the T cells was judged too close to the axis, and the voltage of YG780-A was raised from 641 V to 703 V. PBMC_D858 and all later tubes were recorded with the new voltage. Recorded again with the new voltage before continuing: the PE-Cy7 capture-bead tube (same tube, ctrl_PECy7_rerun.fcs) and the HLA-DR FMO tube (same tube, fmo_HLADR_rerun.fcs). No other control was recorded again.\"\n\nWait, what does gating SOP section 3 say?\n\"3. Compensation acceptance for the HLA-DR read-out\nThe FMO tube contains no HLA-DR reagent, so whatever remains in the HLA-DR detector after compensation is background, and lymphocyte populations that differ only in the other reagents they carry must not differ in it. The compensation used for a group of samples is accepted for reporting if, in the FMO tube recorded with the same fluorescence-detector voltages and compensated with the same matrix, the median compensated HLA-DR-detector signals of CD4_T, CD8_T, DN_T, B_cells and NK_cells lie within 50 detector units of each other (largest minus smallest of the five medians). A compensation that does not meet this criterion has to be corrected before the HLA-DR populations are reported.\"\n\nAnd what does the task prompt say?\n\"1. For every sample in samples.tsv, determine the spillover matrix that describes the signals in its file.\n2. For every sample in samples.tsv, identify every population of gating_sop.md as defined there and report its size.\nRequired outputs under /task/output:\n- spillover.csv - comma-separated. Header: sample, fluorochrome, followed by the eight fluorescence detectors named as in panel.tsv. Exactly one row for every sample_id of samples.tsv and every fluorochrome of panel.tsv (names as written there). The eight rows of a sample are the spillover matrix of that sample: the entry in row f, column d is the signal that fluorochrome f, as it is present on the cells of that sample, produces in detector d at the detector settings with which that sample was recorded, as a fraction of the signal it produces in its own primary detector at the same settings (background-free; the entry of each fluorochrome in its own detector is 1).\n- frequencies.tsv - tab-separated with the columns sample, population, parent, count, percent_of_parent...\"\n\nLet's check what the single-stain controls are, how the spillover matrix should be calculated, and what is currently in $SPILLOVER.",
  "plan": "Inspect $SPILLOVER in PBMC_D617 and other files, check voltages across all files, and look at single stain controls.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat << 'EOF' > inspect_details.py
import fcsparser

meta, data = fcsparser.parse('/task/input/fcs/PBMC_D617.fcs', reformat_meta=False)
print("Keys in PBMC_D617:", [k for k in meta.keys() if 'SPILL' in k])
print("$SPILLOVER value:", repr(meta.get('$SPILLOVER')))

# check detectors / channels
pnn = {k: v for k, v in meta.items() if k.startswith('$P') and k.endswith('N')}
pns = {k: v for k, v in meta.items() if k.startswith('$P') and k.endswith('S')}
print("PNN:", pnn)
print("PNS:", pns)

# check voltages in all files
for f in ['PBMC_D617.fcs', 'PBMC_D858.fcs', 'ctrl_PECy7.fcs', 'ctrl_PECy7_rerun.fcs']:
    m, _ = fcsparser.parse('/task/input/fcs/' + f, reformat_meta=False)
    volts = {k: v for k, v in m.items() if 'VOLT' in k.upper()}
    print(f, volts)

EOF
python3 inspect_details.py
",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}
